                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is one of those games, that very much wants to try to move into the niche market of warship arcade games. It gets some things right, and many things very wrong.  Let's start off with what they get right first.\n\nThe game reasonably well represents modern warships, from small corvettes, to more advanced guided missile cruisers and destroyers.  Complete with actual missiles, which is something no other company has really done.  That's actually a nice change from the standard fare of games, when it comes to warship combat, so that's a plus.\n\nAnd... that's it.  That's what's right.\n\nNow, onto what's wrong.\n\nThe first, and most glaring problem the game has is its graphics.  Though rendered in 3d, everything has a very cheap, if not cartoonish feel. The ship models look like something plucked right from the early 2000's, with horrible models and even worse graphics and texture maps.  That alone could be forgivable, if the game was at least marginally playable.\n\nNow, don't get me wrong, you CAN play it, but the other problems the game has makes that a chore in itself.\n\nAnother problem is the manner in which the game has you the player partake in battle.  The overall battle areas are actually rather small, and in some game modes it's not uncommon to spawn in right on top of an enemy ship.  As if that's not frustrating enough, you have zero spawn protection. Meaning that you're fair game, and can be sunk before you have a chance to do a single thing about it.\n\nThe smaller map areas make for really short engagements, so those missiles, while they'd be cool to really see in action; well they kinda look silly. You go from having the potential to engage a target you can barely even see, to picking someone that's well within gun range and nuking that poor sod from orbit.  \n\nMonetization is also always in your face. I mean, seriously in your face. Every time you head into battle, you'll see these stupid little exclamation marks over parts of your ship. Normally, that'd mean you forgot to do something right? Not here, it's just a reminder that to get whatever special camo, super weapon (missiles), or decal the game wants to sell you. What makes it worse, though, is you can't make this go away.  The company really wants you to sink money into this game, so every chance they get, they'll remind you that to get anywhere in the game, you really need to pay.\n\nThink I'm kidding?  Well if you've played any other of the ship arcade games, you know that when you purchase an upgrade to the ship, it's mounted right away.  Not here. Here, you buy the upgrade, and then have to wait X amount of time for it to be mounted. You can get it right away of course, if you're willing to shell out some premium money to get it.  \n\nThis game really reeks of something that we've all come to loathe. Not just the money hungry pay to win aspect, but from the graphics, to the gameplay, and right down to how upgrades are handled, it's little better than a mobile game.  In fact, I would not be at all surprised if that's just what this is. A mobile game roughly (emphasis on roughly) ported to PC.\n\nOh, and one more thing, if you do play this, you better like 800 X 600 resolution, as you can't change the resolution of the game, and that's a rough guess of whatever its native display is at.\n\nEven Steel ocean, for all its faults, was better than this.  So, while yeah, it's modern warships... everything else makes this a hard pass. Not even worth the trouble.",
